                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "The game is meant to be a fast-paced reaction game with energetic music and a simplistic design, highly similar to Super Hexagon. And on a presentational front, it succeeds. Unlike Super Hexagon, however, the gameplay is conceptually flawed. (In other words, it sucks.) Where Super Hexagon almost continually presents a fair challenge, Tetradecagon  presents the player with an illusion of skill-based play without any form of a fair challenge.\n\nThe game concept seems fine at first: rotate the outer ribs of different polygonic shapes to keep a ball inside from bouncing out in an interesting combination of pong and juggling. However, the concept ends up falling flat for a few reasons.\nA relatively minor problem is that the polygon spins really fast because that's the only way the game can get even remotely close to allowing you to catch the ball, which also moves extremely fast. This speed makes angling shots off of the rib pretty much impossible. That becomes a problem when the only way you can repeatedly juggle the ball is by knocking it straight to the other side ; if you knock it at too much of an angle (lets say, to the rib next to the one you hit it from), the path is so short that there is actually no way to prevent game-overing.\nThe more severe problem is that the rib(s) of the polygon that you're supposed to hit the ball with, change(s) with every strike at the ball, with the new rib being chosen at at random. This takes valuable time to register in your mind, time that you therefore lose to actually turn the ball in time and means that there's a high change that the new rib spawns in a location that's on the exact opposite end of the polygon from where the ball is headed. It can actually be impossible to do the 180 turn required to catch it on the other side because the pall's path through the polygon is more efficient than your speed of spinning the polygon. And even if you do manage such a clutch turn, the previous problem kicks in: it's likely that you didn't have the time to aim this shot, which means it's likely to knock the ball back at a completely indefensible angle.\n\nConsequently, you are not guaranteed to be able to win at all. You can get a series of easy rib spawns and then get screwed by a series of two bad spawns that make you game over.\nThat's bad game design. And it's a real shame, because the concept seems clever; it's just not executable in any realistic way",
                        "timestamp_created": 1499634905,
                        "timestamp_updated": 1499634905,
                        "voted_up": false,
                        "votes_up": 7,
                        "votes_funny": 0,
                        "weighted_vote_score": 0.6045788526535034,
                        "comment_count": 0,
                        "steam_purchase": true,
                        "received_for_free": false,
                        "written_during_early_access": false,
                        "developer_response": "A very well written review, and as the developer and designer of the game I won't hesitate to say it glosses over some of the downfalls I made while creating Tetradecagon. I, nor did my play testers see any problems in it's core mechanics; there is a significant level of tricky (honestly sometimes impossible shots) that the random number generator throws at you, however. \n\nLike most successful games from the twitch genre, Tetradecagon provides both \"checkpoints\" at significant and frequent areas of progression and it reloads to your last \"checkpoint\" as fast as you can hit the spacebar button. Many people who finish this game have far over a thousand deaths. Many of these deaths are due to the twitch nature of the game, which is found in the genre as a whole. I cannot deny that some of them are due to impossible circumstances such as 180 degree flips (these flips can usually be performed will enough reaction time.) Nevertheless, that's simply just how the game was made.\n\nI still encourage people to try the game for themselves.\n\n\n",
                        "timestamp_dev_responded": 1500843120,
                        "primarily_steam_deck": false,
                        "refunded": false
                    }
